import { DocsPage, Demo, Code, ApiReference } from "../../shell.rx"
import { PreviewCardHero } from "../../demos/preview-card/hero.rx"

export default component Page() {
  <DocsPage title="Preview Card" subtitle="A card that previews what a link points to while the link is hovered or focused.">
    <Demo source="preview-card/hero"><PreviewCardHero /></Demo>

    <h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>
    <p>Import the parts and put them together:</p>
    <Code file="preview-card/anatomy.rx.txt" />

    <h2 id="platform">What the browser does</h2>
    <p>
      The card is an element with <code>popover="manual"</code>, shown with{" "}
      <code>{"showPopover({ source: link })"}</code>, which makes the link its anchor. The browser provides:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>The top layer: the card is drawn above everything else, without a portal or a <code>z-index</code>.</li>
      <li>Placement against the link with CSS anchor positioning, flipped to the other side when it doesn't fit.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Popovers that open on hover (<code>interestfor</code>, <code>popover="hint"</code>) aren't in every engine
      yet, and a manual popover gets no light dismiss, so the library adds opening after a delay on hover and on
      keyboard focus, keeping the card open while the pointer is over it, closing on <kbd>Escape</kbd> and on a
      press outside, and <code>data-side</code> with the arrow's position.
    </p>
    <div class="note">
      <p>
        The card is supplementary: the link works without it. So the card takes no role and focus never moves into
        it on its own; the card stays open while focus is on something inside it. It stays in the document while
        closed and is the positioned element itself, so there are no Portal or Positioner parts.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="examples">Examples</h2>

    <h3 id="delays">Delays</h3>
    <p>
      The card opens once the pointer has rested on the link, or focus has stayed on it, for <code>delay</code>{" "}
      milliseconds (600 by default). It closes <code>closeDelay</code> milliseconds (300 by default) after the
      pointer has left both the link and the card, so the pointer can cross the gap; coming back before then keeps
      it open. Focus leaving both closes it too.
    </p>

    <h3 id="paragraphs">In a paragraph</h3>
    <p>
      The card is a <code>{"<div>"}</code>, which can't sit inside a <code>{"<p>"}</code>. The root renders no
      element, so put it around the paragraph and the card after it, as the demo does.
    </p>

    <h3 id="controlled">Controlling it</h3>
    <p>
      Pass <code>open</code> and <code>onOpenChange</code> to the root to hold the state yourself. Hover, focus,{" "}
      <kbd>Escape</kbd> and a press outside then call <code>onOpenChange</code> with the state they ask for.
    </p>

    <h3 id="placement">Placement and the arrow</h3>
    <p>
      The card sits below the link, centred, unless <code>side</code> and <code>align</code> say otherwise;{" "}
      <code>sideOffset</code> is the gap. When the browser flips it, its <code>data-side</code> changes to the side
      it ended up on. <code>PreviewCardArrow</code> is placed on the edge facing the link, centred on the link.
    </p>

    <h3 id="animation">Animating it</h3>
    <p>
      Animate the card in with <code>@starting-style</code> under <code>:popover-open</code>. The library hides it
      as soon as it closes, so animating it out is left to CSS: transition <code>display</code> and{" "}
      <code>overlay</code> with <code>allow-discrete</code>, which takes effect in the engines that support it and
      is ignored in the others.
    </p>

    <h2 id="api-reference">API reference</h2>
    <ApiReference family="preview-card" />
  </DocsPage>
}
